As a Technical Lead, looking at this pipeline failure, we are dealing with two intersecting issues: a strict serialization enforcement by Jackson, and your new requirement to transition the API contract to the DD/MM/YYYY standard.
The UnrecognizedPropertyException you are seeing on /create-batch-from-cache is happening because we injected postingDate into the React payload in the previous step, but the specific Java DTO mapped to that endpoint does not have this property defined. Jackson receives an unknown field and immediately aborts with a 500 error.
Here is the complete, full-stack architectural fix to resolve the crash, prevent future JSON mapping failures, and enforce the DD/MM/YYYY format across the wire.
### 1. The Backend DTO Fix (Resolving the 500 Error)
You need to add the postingDate field to the DTO that is handling the /create-batch-from-cache request. Furthermore, we must instruct Jackson exactly how to parse this new format, and tell it to ignore stray fields so it doesn't crash the server.
Locate the DTO used in your @PostMapping("/create-batch-from-cache") endpoint and update it:
```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonFormat;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonIgnoreProperties;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@Data
@JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true) // Architectural best practice: Prevents 500 errors if frontend sends extra fields
public class CreateBatchFromCacheRequest {
    
    private String requestId;
    private String commonBatchRemarks;
    
    // Enforce the exact DD/MM/YYYY format during JSON deserialization
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.STRING, pattern = "dd/MM/yyyy")
    private LocalDate postingDate;
}

```
